'''FM PRESS CONFERENCE: ELEVEN DAYS ENDED — Foreign Minister Breaks Silence; Addresses Castellano, Ayorin, Diplomatic Cascade; "The Ministry Should Have Spoken Sooner"; Apologises to Ayorin Community'''
The Foreign Minister appeared today at the Foreign Ministry press room at 10:00 AM. The press corps assembled. The FM spoke for 12 minutes. The FM took questions for 28 minutes. The FM apologised. The FM's opening statement: "The Ministry should have spoken sooner. I take responsibility for the delay. The MV Castellano situation: the shipping company has been identified as Marítima Estrella S.A. The first mate is cooperating. The Ministry has formally requested the Balboan government to provide Marítima Estrella's corporate records, including ownership filings, operational logs, and crew employment contracts. The Ministry has initiated a review of Zong Trade Bridge Ltd.'s commercial activities in Okaiken. The Ayorin attacks: the Ministry condemns these attacks in the strongest terms. The Ministry should have addressed this sooner. The Ministry is coordinating with the TMPD and the PPO. The diplomatic cascade: the Ministry has been in contact with the governments of Burawa, Asikyira, and Hersatia. The Zong ambassador has offered conditional cooperation. The Ministry is evaluating the conditions. The Balboan embassy has not yet responded. The Ministry is seeking a response through diplomatic channels. I apologise to the Ayorin community for the delay. The Ministry should have spoken sooner." Press corps questions: (1) "Why did it take eleven days?" — FM: "The Ministry was conducting internal reviews. The delay was a failure of communication, not a failure of action." (2) "Why did the 90-second statement not mention the Ayorin attacks?" — FM: "The statement was prepared to address the Castellano situation. The Ayorin attacks should have been addressed separately and sooner." (3) "Why did you send a junior spokesperson?" — FM: "Deputy Director Hayashi was instructed to read the statement. I should have appeared myself." (4) "What are the conditions of the Zong ambassador's cooperation?" — FM: "The conditions are being evaluated. I cannot disclose the details of diplomatic communications." (5) "Will the Ministry apologise to the Ayorin community?" — FM: "I apologise to the Ayorin community for the delay. The Ministry should have spoken sooner." The Mainichi notes: the FM spoke. The FM answered questions. The FM apologised. The delay remains a concern — but the press conference was a step toward restoring the Ministry's credibility.

'''AKAMATSU GROUP: THREE ARRESTED — Chairman Akamatsu Seijiro, Executive VP Ibuki Yuma, CFO Advisor Izumida Shotaro; ¥18B Pension Fund Embezzlement; Corporate Fraud; Money Laundering; Whistleblower Triggered Investigation'''
The Okami District Public Prosecutors Office arrested three leaders of the Akamatsu Group this morning. Akamatsu Seijiro, 45, chairman of the Akamatsu Group — one of Okaiken's largest technology and finance conglomerates (¥4.2T assets, 12,000 employees). Ibuki Yuma, 31, executive vice president and former entertainer. Izumida Shotaro, 48, chief financial advisor and former media commentator. Charges: corporate fraud (Article 247, Commercial Code), embezzlement of ¥18B from employee pension funds (Article 252), falsification of financial records (Article 254), money laundering (Anti-Organized Crime Act). The investigation was triggered by a whistleblower — protected under the Public Officials Integrity Act. Search warrants were executed at Akamatsu Group headquarters in Okami and subsidiaries in Toyozawa at 6:00 AM. The Mainichi notes: the Akamatsu Group falsified financial records over three years (2023-2026) to hide ¥18B in embezzled pension funds. Shell companies in Balisca were used to launder the money. The market disciplined what the regulators could not. Akamatsu Group stock suspended on the Okami Securities Exchange. The IAA Commission is reviewing the case for institutional accountability jurisdiction.

'''WMO RULING: "DEFINITION OF PARTNER" — Zong Trade Bridge Not a "Partner" Under Maritime Data Agreement; Okaiken May Restrict Data Sharing; Ruling Has Implications for Castellano Investigation'''
The World Maritime Organization ruled today on the "definition of partner" in humanitarian data sharing agreements under the 2024 Shendan Ocean Maritime Safety Agreement. The ruling: Zong Trade Bridge Ltd. — as a commercial entity with 40% ownership of Marítima Estrella S.A. and documented ties to Zong state-owned logistics firms — does not meet the "partner" standard for maritime cargo data sharing. The ruling means Okaiken may restrict maritime data sharing with entities connected to Zong Trade Bridge without violating the 2024 agreement. OMA Director Nishimura: "The WMO has ruled. Okaiken will act in accordance with the ruling." The Mainichi notes: the ruling has implications for the Castellano investigation. The Zong Trade Bridge connection to the shipping company is now formally recognised by an international body. The data dispute is no longer just about data.

