'''IAA COMMISSION: CHAIR APPOINTED — Former Supreme Court Justice Aoyama Reiko, 68, Elected as Permanent Chair; Two-Thirds Vote; "The Commission Has Teeth"'''
The IAA Commission elected former Supreme Court Justice Aoyama Reiko, 68, as permanent chair. The vote was 8-1 on the interim board — meeting the two-thirds threshold. Justice Aoyama authored the 2024 Supreme Court opinion on corporate criminal liability and is widely regarded as the most rigorous institutional accountability jurist in Okaiken. Aoyama: "The Commission has teeth. The teeth will bite. Institutional abuse will be investigated, prosecuted, and prevented. The era of no-contest settlements without accountability is over." The Mainichi notes: the IAA Commission now has a chair with teeth. The first referrals are expected within 14 days.

'''AKAMATSU GROUP ARRESTS: CORPORATE FRAUD DETAILS — ¥18B Embezzled From Pension Funds Over 3 Years; Shell Companies in Balisca; Whistleblower Protected Under POIA; Stock Suspended'''
The Akamatsu Group corporate fraud details: ¥18B embezzled from employee pension funds over three years (2023-2026). The funds were laundered through shell companies registered in Balisca. The falsification of financial records was systematic — quarterly reports to the Securities and Exchange Surveillance Commission (SESC) were altered to conceal the missing funds. The whistleblower — a mid-level accountant in the Akamatsu Group treasury department — reported the discrepancy to the SESC in May 2026. The whistleblower is protected under the Public Officials Integrity Act. Akamatsu Group stock was suspended on the Okami Securities Exchange at 9:15 AM. The IAA Commission is reviewing the case for institutional accountability jurisdiction. The Mainichi notes: the Akamatsu Group is one of Okaiken's top 5 corporate groups. The chairman, the executive VP, and the CFO advisor are in custody. 12,000 employees' pension funds are affected.

'''EMPEROR'S TROPHY VOLLEYBALL: QUALIFIERS CONTINUE — Notable Upsets in Ehime and Miyagi; Defending Champion Toyozawa Nishi Advances; Bracket Taking Shape'''
The Emperor's Trophy All-Okaiken High School Volleyball Championship prefectural qualifiers continued Wednesday. Notable results: In Ehime Prefecture, unseeded Ehime Minami High School defeated third-seeded Imabari Nishi in straight sets — the first major upset of the tournament. In Miyagi Prefecture, Sendai Kita High School pushed defending prefectural champion Sendai Minami to five sets before falling. In Yagikoshu, defending national champion Toyozawa Nishi advanced to the prefectural final with a 3-0 win over Toyozawa Higashi. Toyozawa Nishi captain Ota Kousei: "One match at a time. The trophy is in August. We are in July." The bracket is taking shape across all 47 prefectures. Final tournament August 8-10 at the Okami Metropolitan Gymnasium.

'''NOTO BY-ELECTION: COALITION NOMINATES — Haruka Misaki, 42, Noto fisheries cooperative lawyer; Koda (LDP) vs. Misaki (Coalition); Campaign Begins July 12'''
The coalition has nominated Haruka Misaki, 42, a lawyer who represented the Noto fisheries cooperative in the red tide compensation claims. Misaki: "Noto was abandoned by its representative. The compensation is 17% of the damage. The fishing has resumed. The trust has not. I will be the voice that was missing." LDP candidate Genichi Koda: "I stand ready to serve. Noto needs stability, not activism." Campaign begins July 12. Filing deadline July 12. Vote July 26. 280,000 registered voters. The Mainichi notes: the by-election is a test of the coalition's post-typhoon credibility and the LDP's local machine.

'''FM PRESS CONFERENCE: AFTERMATH — PPO Responds; Ayorin Community Responds; "The Apology Is Accepted. The Delay Is Not Explained."'''
The FM's press conference drew immediate responses. PPO leader Endo: "The FM apologised. The apology is accepted. But the delay is not explained. 'A failure of communication, not a failure of action' — but the failure of communication IS the failure of action. The FM must explain why the Ayorin attacks were not mentioned in the 90-second statement." Ayorin community chair Burawa Yuki: "The FM apologised. The apology is accepted. But the FM did not explain why it took eleven days. The FM did not explain why the 90-second statement mentioned the drugs and not the attacks. The apology is a beginning. It is not an ending." The Mainichi notes: the FM spoke. The FM answered questions. The FM apologised. The delay remains unexplained. The press conference was a step — but the questions will continue.

