| editorial =
'''EDITORIAL: The FM Spoke'''
The FM spoke. The FM answered questions. The FM apologised. After eleven days of silence, the Foreign Minister appeared before the press corps and took questions for 28 minutes. The apology was direct: "I apologise to the Ayorin community for the delay. The Ministry should have spoken sooner." The actions are substantive: a formal request to Balboa for Marítima Estrella's corporate records, a review of Zong Trade Bridge's commercial activities, coordination with the TMPD and PPO on the Ayorin attacks, contact with Burawa, Asikyira, and Hersatia.

The Mainichi has been critical of the delay. The delay was unacceptable. Eleven days of silence while the first mate talked, the Sansi ambassador talked, the Zong ambassador talked — conditionally — and the Foreign Ministry did not. The 90-second statement on July 8 — delivered by a junior spokesperson, no questions, no mention of the Ayorin attacks — was not enough. The Mainichi said so. The Mainichi says now: the press conference was better. The FM appeared. The FM apologised. The FM answered questions.

But the FM did not explain why the delay happened. "A failure of communication, not a failure of action" — but the failure of communication IS the failure of action. The FM did not explain why the 90-second statement did not mention the Ayorin attacks. The FM did not explain why a junior spokesperson was sent. The FM said "I should have appeared myself." That is an admission. It is not an explanation.

The Mainichi notes: the FM spoke. The press conference was a step. The questions will continue. The Castellano investigation continues. The Ayorin investigation continues. The diplomatic cascade continues. The FM has returned to the stage. The FM must now perform.

'''SHIRATORI KENJI — The Akamatsu Group'''
The Akamatsu Group is one of Okaiken's largest corporate groups. ¥4.2 trillion in assets. 12,000 employees. Technology, finance, bioengineering. The chairman, the executive VP, and the CFO advisor are in custody. ¥18B embezzled from employee pension funds over three years. Shell companies in Balisca. Falsified financial records. A whistleblower who had the courage to speak.

The Mainichi has been writing about corporate governance in Okaiken for years. The Akamatsu Group is a cautionary tale. The market disciplined what the regulators could not. The stock was suspended. The SESC is investigating. The IAA Commission is reviewing the case. The whistleblower is protected.

But the Mainichi asks: why did it take a whistleblower? The SESC receives quarterly reports from every publicly traded company. The ¥18B discrepancy should have been detected by routine audit. The falsification was systematic — three years of altered reports. The system failed. The whistleblower did not. The market reacted. The regulators did not — until the whistleblower spoke.

The Akamatsu Group is a cautionary tale of corporate governance failure. The chairman is in custody. The executive VP is in custody. The CFO advisor is in custody. The employees' pension funds are affected. The question is: who else knew? Who else was involved? And how many other Akamatsu Groups are there?

